{"challenges":[{"attack":"The quoted premise might already establish rationality rather than only describe a claimed proof.","claim_id":"displayed-argument","outcome":"survives","result":"The question only reports that a proof claims rationality and describes its steps; it does not assert that the conclusion is true.","tool_ids":[]},{"attack":"Check whether the least-denominator construction assumes a representation before rationality has been assumed.","claim_id":"lowest-terms-representation","outcome":"survives","result":"Within the temporary rationality assumption, a nonzero integer denominator exists; changing both signs makes it positive, so the set of positive denominators is nonempty.","tool_ids":[]},{"attack":"Check whether squaring or multiplying by b^2 divides by zero.","claim_id":"squared-equation","outcome":"survives","result":"The selected denominator is positive, hence b^2 is nonzero. Squaring and multiplication preserve the equality.","tool_ids":[]},{"attack":"Check whether an even square necessarily implies an even integer.","claim_id":"a-is-even","outcome":"fails","result":"The conclusion is mathematically correct, but the proof step invokes a parity decomposition justified by an unproved division-algorithm result. Thus this draft does not establish the claim under its stated derivation basis.","tool_ids":[]},{"attack":"Check whether substitution yields b^2=2c^2 without an invalid cancellation.","claim_id":"b-is-even","outcome":"fails","result":"The algebraic cancellation of 2 is valid, but the subsequent inference that b is even again relies on the unestablished parity-decomposition step.","tool_ids":[]},{"attack":"Check whether 2 is a prohibited common divisor and whether the reductio scope is discharged.","claim_id":"lowest-terms-contradiction","outcome":"fails","result":"If both parity conclusions were established, 2 would contradict the no-common-divisor property and discharge the assumption. In this draft those parity conclusions have an unsupported derivation basis.","tool_ids":[]},{"attack":"Check whether the conclusion is inferred only after the temporary rationality assumption is discharged.","claim_id":"irrationality-conclusion","outcome":"fails","result":"The reductio structure is correctly arranged, but its contradiction depends on the unsupported parity derivation; the central conclusion is therefore not fully established by this submitted proof record.","tool_ids":[]}],"checks":[{"basis_reasoning":"The statement is directly reported in the question, including the lowest-terms setup and the conclusion that both integers are even.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":[],"claim_id":"displayed-argument","reasoning":"Supported as an attribution to the question premise, not as proof that the argument's claimed conclusion is true.","verdict":"supported"},{"basis_reasoning":"This is a definition used in square-equation; no external source is needed for a definitional convention.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":[],"claim_id":"root-definition","reasoning":"The definition is suitable for the stated use.","verdict":"supported"},{"basis_reasoning":"These are definitions used to interpret rationality, evenness, and common divisors.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":[],"claim_id":"integer-definitions","reasoning":"The definitions are adequate for the proof steps that use them.","verdict":"supported"},{"basis_reasoning":"The named well-ordering principle is correctly stated and explicitly applied in least-denominator. However parity-decomposition does not supply a proof of the division algorithm from that principle.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["least-denominator","parity-decomposition"],"claim_id":"well-ordering-principle","reasoning":"The theorem supports selecting least b. It does not by itself validate the asserted unshown derivation of the division algorithm in parity-decomposition.","verdict":"conditional"},{"basis_reasoning":"Its stated basis is derivation, but parity-decomposition merely asserts that the division algorithm is proved from well-ordering; no such proof steps are present. A separate division algorithm theorem was not named as a standard result.","basis_verdict":"unsupported","checked_step_ids":["parity-decomposition"],"claim_id":"parity-dichotomy","reasoning":"The dichotomy is true, but it is not established by the supplied self-contained derivation or by a properly recorded separate standard-result application.","verdict":"unsupported"},{"basis_reasoning":"This is a temporary local assumption. Its scope lists every step through lowest-terms-conflict that uses it, and lowest-terms-conflict is identified as its discharge step.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["rational-representation","positive-denominator","least-denominator","lowest-terms-property","square-equation","a-square-even","a-even","substitute-a","b-square-even","b-even","lowest-terms-conflict"],"claim_id":"rationality-assumption","reasoning":"The local-assumption scope and intended discharge are structurally correct, although the discharge's substantive support is incomplete because of the parity issue.","verdict":"conditional"},{"basis_reasoning":"From rational-representation and positive-denominator, the denominator set is nonempty; least-denominator applies well-ordering. lowest-terms-property correctly shows that a common g>1 produces a smaller positive denominator.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["rational-representation","positive-denominator","least-denominator","lowest-terms-property"],"claim_id":"lowest-terms-representation","reasoning":"The construction avoids assuming an arbitrary fraction was initially in lowest terms and establishes the required coprimality property.","verdict":"supported"},{"basis_reasoning":"square-equation follows from the selected representation, the square-root definition, and b>0, so multiplication by b^2 is legitimate.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["square-equation"],"claim_id":"squared-equation","reasoning":"The equation a^2=2b^2 is correctly derived under the local assumption.","verdict":"supported"},{"basis_reasoning":"The claim is labeled derivation, but a-even depends on parity-decomposition, whose stated derivation is not supplied.","basis_verdict":"unsupported","checked_step_ids":["a-square-even","a-even","parity-decomposition"],"claim_id":"a-is-even","reasoning":"a-square-even is established, and the odd-square calculation is correct conditional on the parity dichotomy. The required dichotomy is not established in this record.","verdict":"unsupported"},{"basis_reasoning":"The claim depends on a-is-even and parity-decomposition; both parity inferences inherit the unsupported derivation basis.","basis_verdict":"unsupported","checked_step_ids":["substitute-a","b-square-even","b-even","parity-decomposition"],"claim_id":"b-is-even","reasoning":"The substitution and cancellation are valid, but the final parity inference is unsupported in the recorded proof.","verdict":"unsupported"},{"basis_reasoning":"The conflict step correctly uses the least-terms property and would contradict it if a-even and b-even were established. Those prerequisite claims are not established here.","basis_verdict":"unsupported","checked_step_ids":["lowest-terms-conflict","a-even","b-even"],"claim_id":"lowest-terms-contradiction","reasoning":"The contradiction is conditional on the missing parity result, rather than an established derivation in this draft.","verdict":"unsupported"},{"basis_reasoning":"The reductio form is appropriate: conclude-irrational depends on the stated discharge step. But that discharge relies on the unsupported parity derivation.","basis_verdict":"unsupported","checked_step_ids":["lowest-terms-conflict","conclude-irrational"],"claim_id":"irrationality-conclusion","reasoning":"The intended conclusion is mathematically correct, and the original claimed rationality conclusion is invalid; nevertheless this proof record needs the parity theorem proved or separately and properly invoked before it establishes irrationality.","verdict":"unsupported"}],"missing_evidence":["A self-contained proof of the division algorithm/parity dichotomy from the well-ordering principle, or a separately named standard-result claim for the division algorithm with explicit application steps."],"recommended_action":"revise","tool_requests":[]}
